.TH CUSTODIO 1 "" "custodio" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
custodio \- import, list and delete keys of the custodio signing service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B custodio import
[\fB\-\-id\fR \fIkey id\fR]
.I file.p12
.br
.B custodio list
[\fIkey id\fR]
.br
.B custodio delete
.I key id
.br
.B custodio register
[\fB\-\-module\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-remove\fR | \fB\-\-refresh\-only\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B custodio
is the command-line tool of the custodio signing service.
.B import
reads a PKCS#12 file and stores its key where the service keeps keys.
The file password is asked on the terminal.
It is never taken from the arguments or from the environment.
When the import fails because of the file, the error says what to do:
a wrong password (the file has an integrity check that does not match) asks you to retype it;
a file that is not readable PKCS#12, or that does not hold exactly one private key with its certificate,
asks you to export it again (the README shows how from Firefox);
a file without an integrity check that does not decrypt could be either, and the error says both.
After a successful import
.B custodio
says that Firefox and any other running program that loaded the module see the new key only after they restart.
.PP
.B list
shows one row for each of your keys, in order of key id, with these columns:
the
.I id
(the key id),
the
.I type
(\fBrsa2048\fR or \fBecdsa\-p256\fR),
the storage
.I kind
(\fBtpm\fR, \fBencrypted\-tpm\fR or \fBencrypted\-host\fR),
the
.I subject
of its certificate, and the end of its validity
(the certificate's notAfter, in UTC).
The subject is shown as the common name of the certificate, or as the whole subject (RFC 4514) if it has no
readable common name.
If any key is
.B encrypted\-tpm
or
.BR encrypted\-host ,
one note at the foot says that these kinds are weaker than
.B tpm
and points to the README.
If a certificate cannot be read, the key is still listed, its subject and end of validity
are shown as "certificate unreadable", and the exit status is 1.
Control characters and other characters that could change the screen in any of these texts
are shown as \fB\\x\fINN\fR or \fB\\u{\fINNNN\fB}\fR escapes.
Only your own keys are listed.
If you have none, it prints a sentence saying so and how to import one, and exits with status 0.
.PP
.B list
with a
.I key id
shows that key in full instead: the same fields, and also the whole subject, the issuer, the serial number,
the start of the validity and the SHA\-256 fingerprint of the certificate.
If you have no key with that id, it says so, names
.BR "custodio list" ,
and exits with status 1.
.B list
does not ask for authorization: it never calls polkit.
.PP
After a successful
.B import
or
.BR list ,
the tool asks the service whether its state directory is on an encrypted device.
If the service reports that it is not, or that it could not tell, a warning is printed on standard error:
without full\-disk encryption, whoever takes the machine can use your keys, though they cannot copy a
.B tpm
key.
It points to the section "Full\-disk encryption" of the README.
The warning blocks nothing and does not change what is printed on standard output or the exit status;
nothing is printed when the state directory is encrypted or when the service is too old to say.
.PP
.B delete
deletes your key
.IR "key id" ,
together with its certificate.
Before it calls the service it asks on the terminal and names the key.
The question says that the key cannot be recovered.
Only the answer \fBy\fR or \fByes\fR continues; anything else, an empty answer and the end of input included,
cancels: nothing is deleted and the exit status is 1.
There is no option, environment variable or configuration that skips the confirmation.
It is read from the controlling terminal, never from standard input,
so without a terminal
.B delete
refuses and deletes nothing.
After you confirm, polkit asks for authorization as well; if it is refused, nothing is deleted.
After a successful delete it says that Firefox and any other running program that loaded the module
see the change only after they restart.
.B delete
takes exactly one argument, the key id, and no options;
to delete a key whose id starts with a dash, write
.B \-\-
before it.
.PP
.B register
makes the module visible to the native browsers (Firefox, Thunderbird, LibreWolf, Floorp, Waterfox) of the user who runs it.
It lists their profiles (legacy
.I ~/.mozilla/firefox
and XDG roots) and adds the installed module,
.IR /usr/lib/custodio/libcustodio_pkcs11.so ,
to each with NSS's
.BR modutil (1),
which must be installed (\fBlibnss3\-tools\fR or \fBnss\-tools\fR).
An entry whose library is that file is recognised under any name and never added twice;
an entry for a Custodio module file that no longer exists is updated;
no other entry is touched.
Each profile is edited on a copy, and one file is replaced in one step, so an interruption leaves the old state or the new one.
It prints one line per browser and profile with what it found and did.
A profile whose browser is open is skipped and named: close the browser and run it again.
Those skips and failures make the exit status 1; a system that already loads p11\-kit (nothing to register) does not.
It refuses to run as root or under sudo (exit status 2): it edits the profiles of the user who runs it.
It needs no authorization and does not use the service.
For the Firefox snap, which cannot read
.IR /usr/lib/custodio ,
it also keeps a copy of the module at
.IR ~/snap/firefox/common/libcustodio_pkcs11.so ,
written beside its target and renamed into place (a link at that path is replaced, never followed), and registers that copy in the snap's profiles.
A copy is made only when the snap has a profile.
If a snap profile already has an entry for the installed module, that entry is kept and the copy is still placed, but that profile does not use it:
the copy is not a sign that a profile points to it.
On every run, a copy whose bytes differ from the module is replaced at the same path and reported as
.BR refreshed ;
a run never creates a copy where there was none unless it registers a snap profile.
If a copy cannot be replaced, the command names it and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B \-\-module \fIpath\fR
Register this library instead of the installed one.
It must exist: otherwise one error is printed and nothing is changed.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Remove every entry whose library is a Custodio module file (\fBlibcustodio_pkcs11.so\fR), whatever its name.
This includes an entry added by hand, so it is wider than the entries
.B register
added itself.
The module file need not exist.
It also deletes the snap copy.
If a snap profile is open, that profile keeps its entry and the copy stays, the profile is named, and the exit status is 1.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh\-only
Only replace the snap copy if it exists and its bytes differ from the module's; do nothing else (no profile is read, and
.BR modutil (1)
is not needed).
It never creates a copy, and does nothing, with exit status 0, if there is no installed module.
The outcome (\fBrefreshed\fR, up to date, no copy, no installed module, or the failure and its reason) is written to your journal with the identifier \fBcustodio\-register\fR, at error priority for a failure.
A journal that cannot be reached is reported on standard error and does not change the exit status.
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-remove .
.PP
When standard input is a terminal,
.B import
and
.B delete
start
.B pkttyagent
(with
.B setpriv
from util\-linux, so that it ends if the command is killed) while they wait for polkit,
so that polkit can ask on the terminal when the session has no graphical agent.
The agent is registered for their own process only, as a fallback:
never for another process or for the whole session, and an agent of your session keeps answering.
They stop it when the call returns, whether it succeeded, failed or was interrupted.
If
.B pkttyagent
or
.B setpriv
is missing, no agent is started and polkit answers as it would without one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded; for
.BR list ,
also when there are no keys.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed: the service or the system bus could not be reached, the service answered with an error
(whose message is shown on standard error), a certificate could not be read, you did not confirm a
.BR delete ,
or there is no terminal to confirm it on.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-id " key id"
Name the stored key
.IR "key id" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help of the command, or of the tool when no command is given, on standard output, and exit with status 0.
It is the only argument allowed with the command.
